  "id": "Compound::DB01203 - Gene::155",
  "verbalization": "Nadolol is implicated in the relationship with Adrb3 through a series of interconnected pathways that highlight its role as a beta-adrenergic antagonist. The evidence suggests that Nadolol, through its mechanism of action, leads to the development of Raynaud's Phenomenon or Raynaud's Disease, which are mediated by Propranolol. Propranolol, in turn, binds to Adrb3, establishing a direct interaction between Nadolol and the receptor. This pathway is supported by the lowest common ancestor (LCA) of Nadolol and Propranolol, both classified under the broader category of Beta-Adrenergic Antagonists, reinforcing the shared pharmacological profile.\n\nAdditionally, the evidence points to Nadolol's ability to influence the cardiovascular system, leading to Hypertensive conditions, which are further modulated by Labetalol, another adrenergic antagonist that also binds to Adrb3. While the LCA of Nadolol and Labetalol is categorized under Diastereoisomeric Mixture, this classification does not negate the core mechanism of interaction between Nadolol and Adrb3. The integration of these pathways underscores the multifaceted role of Nadolol in modulating adrenergic signaling, with Adrb3 being a key target in the context of its pharmacological effects.",
